No scores are visible. Every trial trained without errors, so I have to choose using priors about the task. The best guess for the strongest line is r2c1: a footprint-sized nonlinear head on a body with coordinate channels and a global broadcast. r3c2 and r3c3 refined its body. They told us nothing about the head's main job. That job is to decide which layer a brick lands on (the highest filled cell under its footprint) and whether the target has exactly those cells at that layer. That is a product of a landing condition and a match condition, which a ReLU head can only approximate. For this last round I propose:
- (A) r2c1 with a learned head that mixes over layers, weighting a "match" score by a learned landing distribution;
- (B) r2c1 that also gives the raw observation directly to the footprint head, so per-cell detail is not blurred by the 3x3 convs;
- (C) the same mixture head on a 3D encoder whose weights are shared across layers.
A and B change one thing each relative to r2c1.
